(_zk2.chapters = _zk2.chapters || []).push(
  { n:17, summary:"The Lord has Ezekiel pose a riddle for the rebellious house: a great eagle with great wings and many colors came to Lebanon, cropped off the highest branch of the cedar, and carried it into a land of traffick. He took seed of the land and planted it by great waters, where it grew into a low, spreading vine. When a second great eagle appeared, the vine bent its roots toward him. The meaning: the king of Babylon took the king and princes of Jerusalem to Babylon, and set one of the king's seed on the throne under a covenant and an oath. That king rebelled, sending ambassadors into Egypt for horses and much people, so he dies in Babylon, and Pharaoh's army will not save him. The Lord closes with hope: He will take a tender twig from the top of the high cedar and plant it in the mountain of Israel, where it becomes a goodly cedar under which all fowl dwell.",
    context:"A riddle and parable for the rebellious house of Israel, explaining the king of Babylon's capture of Jerusalem's king and the fate of the vassal who broke his oath by turning to Egypt.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 17:14", text:"That the kingdom might be base, that it might not lift itself up, but that by keeping of his covenant it might stand." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 17:19", text:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD; As I live, surely mine oath that he hath despised, and my covenant that he hath broken, even it will I recompense upon his own head." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 17:24", text:"And all the trees of the field shall know that I the LORD have brought down the high tree, have exalted the low tree, have dried up the green tree, and have made the dry tree to flourish: I the LORD have spoken and have done it." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Covenants are sacred; breaking an oath made before God brings judgment, yet the Lord can raise low things to glory.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"In the riddle, what does the first great eagle crop off from the cedar of Lebanon?", choices:["The highest branch and top of its young twigs","Its roots and the soil around them","A single leaf from a low bough","Its ripe fruit in baskets"], a:0, why:"The eagle came to Lebanon, took the highest branch of the cedar, cropped off the top of his young twigs, and carried it into a land of traffick." },
      { q:"Whom do the two great eagles represent in the Lord's explanation?", choices:["Pharaoh of Egypt and the prince of Tyrus","The kings of Assyria and Persia","The king of Babylon and Pharaoh of Egypt","The princes of Ammon and Moab"], a:2, why:"The explanation says the king of Babylon came to Jerusalem and carried off its king and princes, and the vine rebelled by sending ambassadors into Egypt for horses and much people." },
      { q:"What specific act brought judgment on the king set over Judah?", choices:["He refused to pay taxes on grain","He despised the oath and broke the covenant he had made with Babylon","He rebuilt the high places of Israel","He burned the temple records"], a:1, why:"He had given his hand to the covenant and oath, but sent ambassadors into Egypt for horses and much people; the Lord said He would recompense His despised oath and broken covenant on his own head." }
    ] },
  { n:18, summary:"The people use a proverb, saying the fathers have eaten sour grapes and the children's teeth are set on edge. The Lord rejects it: every soul is His, and the soul that sinneth, it shall die. Ezekiel sketches three generations. A just man who does what is lawful and right, restores the debtor's pledge, feeds the hungry, and charges no usury shall surely live. His violent son shall surely die for his own sins. That son's child, who sees his father's sins and refuses to copy them, shall not die for his father's iniquity. If the wicked turns from all his sins, his transgressions shall not be mentioned; if the righteous turns away from his righteousness, he shall die. God's way is equal, and Israel's ways are unequal. He takes no pleasure in the death of the wicked: repent, make a new heart and a new spirit, and live.",
    context:"The Lord's answer to the exiles' sour-grapes proverb, teaching individual accountability before God.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 18:2", text:"What mean ye, that ye use this proverb concerning the land of Israel, saying, The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge?" },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 18:20", text:"The soul that sinneth, it shall die. The son shall not bear the iniquity of the father, neither shall the father bear the iniquity of the son: the righteousness of the righteous shall be upon him, and the wickedness of the wicked shall be upon him." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 18:31", text:"Cast away from you all your transgressions, whereby ye have transgressed; and make you a new heart and a new spirit: for why will ye die, O house of Israel?" }
    ],
    takeaway:"Each person answers to God for his or her own choices; whoever repents and turns will live.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which proverb concerning the land of Israel does the Lord refuse to hear anymore?", choices:["The vision is for an appointed time","The fathers have eaten sour grapes, and the children's teeth are set on edge","A house divided cannot stand","The days are long, and every counsel fails"], a:1, why:"The Lord asks what they mean by using this proverb and swears it will no longer be used in Israel, because every soul belongs to Him." },
      { q:"According to the chapter, what happens to a son who sees his father's sins, considers, and does not do such like?", choices:["He shall not die for the iniquity of his father; he shall surely live","He bears half of his father's guilt","He must repay his father's debts","He is cut off until the third generation"], a:0, why:"The chapter says he shall not die for the iniquity of his father but shall surely live, and that the son shall not bear the iniquity of the father." },
      { q:"What does the Lord say He desires instead of the death of the wicked?", choices:["Punishment shared among the whole family","Offerings and sacrifices at the temple","That he return from his ways and live","That he leave the land of Israel"], a:2, why:"The Lord asks whether He has any pleasure that the wicked should die, and not that he should return from his ways and live; the call is to repent and make a new heart and a new spirit." }
    ] },
  { n:19, summary:"Ezekiel takes up a lamentation for the princes of Israel. Their mother was a lioness who raised a whelp that grew into a young lion, learned to catch the prey, and devoured men, until the nations took him in their pit and led him with chains unto the land of Egypt. She raised another whelp; he too devoured men and laid waste their cities, until the nations spread their net, took him in their pit, and brought him in chains to the king of Babylon, so that his voice was heard no more upon the mountains of Israel. Then the mother is pictured as a fruitful vine planted by the waters, with strong rods for the sceptres of those who bore rule. She was plucked up in fury, cast down to the ground, the east wind dried her fruit, and fire consumed her rods, leaving her in a dry wilderness with no strong rod to be a sceptre to rule.",
    context:"A lamentation for the princes of Israel, mourning their downfall through a lioness robbed of her whelps and a vine plucked up and dried.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 19:2", text:"And say, What is thy mother? A lioness: she lay down among lions, she nourished her whelps among young lions." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 19:9", text:"And they put him in ward in chains, and brought him to the king of Babylon: they brought him into holds, that his voice should no more be heard upon the mountains of Israel." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 19:14", text:"And fire is gone out of a rod of her branches, which hath devoured her fruit, so that she hath no strong rod to be a sceptre to rule. This is a lamentation, and shall be for a lamentation." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Power that devours instead of protects ends in captivity; only the rule God gives endures.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Where was the first lion's whelp taken when the nations caught him?", choices:["To the palace at Babylon","Into the temple treasury","With chains unto the land of Egypt","To the mountains of Armenia"], a:2, why:"The nations heard of him, took him in their pit, and brought him with chains unto the land of Egypt." },
      { q:"In the second image, the mother is compared to what, and what happened to it?", choices:["A lily, trampled by cattle","A vine planted by the waters, plucked up in fury and dried by the east wind","A wheat field burned after harvest","An olive tree cut down for timber"], a:1, why:"The mother is like a fruitful vine planted by the waters with strong rods for sceptres, but she was plucked up in fury, cast down, and the east wind dried up her fruit." },
      { q:"What is the meaning of having no strong rod to be a sceptre to rule?", choices:["The land would lack timber for building","The priests would lose their robes of office","The army would have no weapons","The line of ruling princes was cut off, leaving no one to govern"], a:3, why:"Fire out of a rod of her branches devoured her fruit so that no strong rod remained for a sceptre; the lamentation mourns the end of the princes who bore rule." }
    ] },
  { n:20, summary:"In the seventh year, elders of Israel sit before Ezekiel to inquire of the Lord, but He refuses to be inquired of by them. Instead He rehearses their history: He chose Israel in Egypt and swore to bring them into a land flowing with milk and honey, commanding them to cast away the idols of Egypt, but they rebelled. In the wilderness He gave statutes, judgments, and sabbaths as a sign, yet that generation rebelled, and their children after them, so He swore to scatter them among the heathen. In the land they sacrificed on every high hill at the place called Bamah, and still made their sons pass through the fire. He will rule over them with fury poured out, purge out the rebels, and bring a humbled people to serve Him in His holy mountain. The chapter closes with fire kindled in the forest of the south that will not be quenched.",
    context:"Dated to the seventh year of the captivity, when certain elders of Israel came to inquire of the LORD and sat before Ezekiel.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 20:12", text:"Moreover also I gave them my sabbaths, to be a sign between me and them, that they might know that I am the LORD that sanctify them." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 20:32", text:"And that which cometh into your mind shall not be at all, that ye say, We will be as the heathen, as the families of the countries, to serve wood and stone." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 20:44", text:"And ye shall know that I am the LORD, when I have wrought with you for my name's sake, not according to your wicked ways, nor according to your corrupt doings, O ye house of Israel, saith the Lord GOD." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Rebellion repeats through generations, yet God works for His name's sake to purge rebels and gather a people who serve Him.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What name does the Lord say the high place is called unto this day?", choices:["Zion","Gibeah","Migdol","Bamah"], a:3, why:"The Lord asked what is the high place whereunto ye go, and said the name thereof is called Bamah unto this day." },
      { q:"Why does the Lord refuse to be inquired of by the elders at the chapter's opening?", choices:["They and their fathers had polluted His sabbaths and worshiped idols from Egypt onward","They lacked the proper offerings with them","They came on the wrong day of the month","They had already received an answer through another prophet"], a:0, why:"The elders sat before Ezekiel to inquire, but the Lord rehearsed how Israel had rebelled in Egypt, in the wilderness, and in the land, making their sons pass through the fire; therefore He said, I will not be inquired of by you." },
      { q:"What did the Lord give Israel in the wilderness to be a sign between Him and them?", choices:["Gold vessels for the camp","A yearly feast of ingathering","His sabbaths, that they might know He sanctifies them","Engraved tablets for each tribe"], a:2, why:"He gave them His statutes and judgments, and also His sabbaths to be a sign between Him and them, that they might know He is the Lord who sanctifies them." }
    ] },
  { n:21, summary:"The Lord draws His sword out of the sheath against Jerusalem and the land of Israel, cutting off both righteous and wicked from the south to the north. Ezekiel must sigh with bitterness before the people, for the tidings make every heart melt and every knee weak as water. A sword is sharpened and furbished to be given into the hand of the slayer; it shall be upon God's people and all the princes of Israel, doubled the third time, its point set against all their gates. Then two ways are appointed for the sword of the king of Babylon: at the parting of the way he uses divination, making his arrows bright, consulting images, and looking in the liver, and the lot at his right hand falls on Jerusalem. The profane wicked prince's diadem and crown come off; the kingdom is overturned until He come whose right it is, and the Lord will give it Him. The chapter ends against the Ammonites, whose lying divinations bring the same sword on them.",
    context:"An oracle against Jerusalem, the land of Israel, and the Ammonites, picturing the Babylonian invasion as the Lord's own sword drawn from the sheath.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 21:5", text:"That all flesh may know that I the LORD have drawn forth my sword out of his sheath: it shall not return any more." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 21:21", text:"For the king of Babylon stood at the parting of the way, at the head of the two ways, to use divination: he made his arrows bright, he consulted with images, he looked in the liver." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 21:27", text:"I will overturn, overturn, overturn, it: and it shall be no more, until he come whose right it is; and I will give it him." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Earthly crowns fall, but the throne belongs to Him whose right it is until He comes to claim it.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How did the king of Babylon seek a decision at the parting of the two ways?", choices:["He waited for a prophet's word","By divination: he made his arrows bright, consulted with images, and looked in the liver","He counted the stars for a sign","He asked the elders of Jerusalem to surrender"], a:1, why:"He stood at the head of the two ways to use divination, making his arrows bright, consulting with images, and looking in the liver; the divination at his right hand was for Jerusalem." },
      { q:"What is announced by the words to remove the diadem and take off the crown?", choices:["A new festival would replace the coronation day","The crown would be polished and restored","The monarchy would be overturned until He come whose right it is","The crown would be sent to Egypt as tribute"], a:2, why:"The profane wicked prince's day had come; the Lord said He would overturn, overturn, overturn, and it should be no more until He come whose right it is, and He will give it Him." },
      { q:"What does the chapter teach about who truly holds the right to rule?", choices:["Earthly crowns are temporary; the throne belongs to the rightful One whom the Lord will crown","Rulers keep power by military strength alone","Divination reveals who should reign","The people's choice establishes the crown forever"], a:0, why:"The diadem is removed and the kingdom overturned until He come whose right it is; God Himself gives the throne to its rightful heir." }
    ] },
  { n:22, summary:"Jerusalem is the bloody city: princes shed blood, children dishonor parents, the stranger is oppressed, the fatherless and widow are vexed, sabbaths are profaned, gifts are taken to shed blood, and usury and extortion are practiced while the Lord is forgotten. Therefore He scatters her among the heathen and consumes her filthiness. The house of Israel has become dross (brass, tin, iron, and lead in the furnace), so the Lord gathers them into Jerusalem and blows the fire of His fury until they melt like silver. Third, the land is not cleansed nor rained upon: her prophets conspire like a roaring lion ravening the prey, her priests put no difference between the holy and profane, her princes are like wolves ravening to shed blood, her prophets daub with untempered mortar, divining lies in God's name, and the people oppress the poor. The Lord sought for a man to stand in the gap and found none, so indignation is poured out and every man's own way recompensed upon his head.",
    context:"Three oracles against the bloody city of Jerusalem: her catalogue of sins, her dross melted in the furnace, and the search for a man to stand in the gap.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 22:18", text:"Son of man, the house of Israel is to me become dross: all they are brass, and tin, and iron, and lead, in the midst of the furnace; they are even the dross of silver." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 22:26", text:"Her priests have violated my law, and have profaned mine holy things: they have put no difference between the holy and profane, neither have they shewed difference between the unclean and the clean, and have hid their eyes from my sabbaths, and I am profaned among them." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 22:30", text:"And I sought for a man among them, that should make up the hedge, and stand in the gap before me for the land, that I should not destroy it: but I found none." }
    ],
    takeaway:"When no one stands in the gap, judgment falls; God calls someone in every generation to defend the vulnerable and keep the holy distinct.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What image does the Lord use for the house of Israel in the furnace?", choices:["Green wood that will not burn","Clay pots baked too hard","Dross, the dross of silver, mixed of brass, tin, iron, and lead","Salt that has lost its savor"], a:2, why:"The Lord says the house of Israel has become dross, all brass, tin, iron, and lead in the midst of the furnace, even the dross of silver, and He will melt them in Jerusalem." },
      { q:"What did the Lord seek among the people before destroying the land?", choices:["Ten righteous strangers","A rebuilding fund for the temple","A prophet to foretell the exact day","A man to make up the hedge and stand in the gap, and He found none"], a:3, why:"The Lord sought for a man who should stand in the gap for the land so He need not destroy it, but He found none, so His indignation was poured out." },
      { q:"What failure does the chapter highlight about the priests?", choices:["They charged too much for sacrifices","They put no difference between the holy and profane and hid their eyes from God's sabbaths","They refused to teach in the temple courts","They wore the wrong garments at the feasts"], a:1, why:"Her priests violated the law, profaned holy things, made no difference between holy and profane or clean and unclean, and hid their eyes from the sabbaths, so God was profaned among them." }
    ] },
  { n:23, summary:"Two sisters, daughters of one mother, turn to idolatry: Aholah is Samaria, the elder, and Aholibah is Jerusalem. Samaria doted on the Assyrians and defiled herself with their idols, so the Lord delivered her into their hands; her children were taken and she fell by the sword. Jerusalem saw this yet grew more corrupt, doting on the Assyrians, then sending messengers to the Babylonians portrayed upon the wall, and longing again for Egypt. Therefore the Lord raises up her alienated lovers (Babylonians, Chaldeans, Pekod, Shoa, and Koa) to judge her, strip her, slay her remnant by the sword, and burn the rest with fire. She must drink her sister's cup, filled with astonishment and desolation, because she forgot the Lord and cast Him behind her back. Both sisters shed blood, caused their sons to pass through the fire to idols, and defiled the sanctuary and sabbaths; judgment will make the lewdness to cease out of the land.",
    context:"An oracle comparing Samaria, called Aholah, and Jerusalem, called Aholibah, to two sisters whose unfaithfulness brings judgment on both.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 23:4", text:"And the names of them were Aholah the elder, and Aholibah her sister: and they were mine, and they bare sons and daughters. Thus were their names; Samaria is Aholah, and Jerusalem Aholibah." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 23:31", text:"Thou hast walked in the way of thy sister; therefore will I give her cup into thine hand." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 23:37", text:"That they have committed adultery, and blood is in their hands, and with their idols have they committed adultery, and have also caused their sons, whom they bare unto me, to pass for them through the fire, to devour them." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Idolatry and trust in worldly powers make God's people share the very judgments they watched others receive.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which two cities do the sisters Aholah and Aholibah represent?", choices:["Samaria and Jerusalem","Jericho and Bethel","Damascus and Tyre","Hebron and Gaza"], a:0, why:"The Lord names them plainly: Samaria is Aholah, the elder sister, and Jerusalem is Aholibah." },
      { q:"What is the cup the Lord gives Aholibah to drink?", choices:["A cup of healing after the siege","Her sister Samaria's cup, the same judgment of conquest and desolation Samaria suffered","A cup of blessing at the altar","A cup of mercy mixed with water"], a:1, why:"Because Aholibah walked in the way of her sister, the Lord gave her Samaria's cup, deep and large, filled with drunkenness and sorrow, with the cup of astonishment and desolation." },
      { q:"According to the final accusation, what made the sisters' guilt so severe?", choices:["They traded with too many nations","They rebuilt idols after destroying them","They refused hospitality to strangers","They shed blood, sacrificed their sons to idols, and defiled the sanctuary and sabbaths"], a:3, why:"They committed adultery with their idols, caused their sons to pass through the fire, and came the same day into the sanctuary after slaying their children to idols, profaning the sabbaths." }
    ] },
  { n:24, summary:"On the day the king of Babylon sets himself against Jerusalem, Ezekiel tells a parable of a boiling pot: choice pieces and bones seethe in it, but the scum never goes out. Woe to the bloody city, whose blood, poured on the top of the rock, is uncovered and cries for vengeance. The fire is made great, the flesh consumed, the bones burned, and the empty pot heated until its scum melts away, because the city was purged and would not be purged. Then the Lord takes away the desire of Ezekiel's eyes with a stroke: his wife dies at evening, and he may not mourn, weep, or eat the bread of men. He does as commanded and explains: the sanctuary, the desire of their eyes, will be profaned, their sons and daughters will fall by the sword, and they will pine away in silence. Ezekiel is their sign; when it comes, their mouth shall be opened and they shall know the Lord.",
    context:"Dated to the ninth year, tenth month, tenth day, the day the king of Babylon set himself against Jerusalem; holds the boiling-pot parable and the sign of Ezekiel's wife.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 24:2", text:"Son of man, write thee the name of the day, even of this same day: the king of Babylon set himself against Jerusalem this same day." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 24:16", text:"Son of man, behold, I take away from thee the desire of thine eyes with a stroke: yet neither shalt thou mourn nor weep, neither shall thy tears run down." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 24:24", text:"Thus Ezekiel is unto you a sign: according to all that he hath done shall ye do: and when this cometh, ye shall know that I am the Lord GOD." }
    ],
    takeaway:"God's warnings always come to pass; even grief can become a sign that turns hearts to know the Lord.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What happened to Ezekiel's wife, and how did he respond?", choices:["She was taken captive, and he followed the army","She recovered after prayer, and he gave thanks publicly","She died at even as the Lord foretold, and he did as he was commanded without outward mourning","She was ill, and he fasted until she recovered"], a:2, why:"The Lord said He would take away the desire of Ezekiel's eyes with a stroke; at even his wife died, and in the morning he did as he was commanded, without mourning." },
      { q:"What did Ezekiel's unexplained restraint in mourning signify for Israel?", choices:["The sanctuary, the desire of their eyes, would be profaned, leaving grief too stunned for mourning","That mourning feasts were now forbidden by law","That the exiles should rejoice instead of grieving","That the dead would be restored at the temple"], a:0, why:"As Ezekiel did, so the exiles would do: the sanctuary, the excellency of their strength and desire of their eyes, would be profaned, their sons and daughters fall by the sword, and they would pine away, mourning one toward another." },
      { q:"In the boiling-pot parable, why does the Lord say the city's blood sits upon the top of a rock?", choices:["So travelers could avoid the place","Because it was not covered with dust, leaving her guilt uncovered to call for vengeance","Because the altar stood on solid stone","So rain would wash it away quickly"], a:1, why:"She poured her blood on the top of the rock and not on the ground to cover it with dust, so the Lord set it there uncovered to bring up fury and take vengeance." }
    ] },
  { n:25, summary:"Four small nations are judged for gloating over Judah's fall. The Ammonites cried Aha when the sanctuary was profaned and clapped their hands with despite, so Rabbah becomes a stable for camels and their land a possession for the men of the east. Moab and Seir sneer that the house of Judah is like all the heathen, so the Lord opens Moab's side from its glory cities (Beth-jeshimoth, Baal-meon, and Kiriathaim), giving its frontier cities to the men of the east. Edom took vengeance on the house of Judah and greatly offended, so the Lord will cut off man and beast and make it desolate from Teman, laying His vengeance on Edom by the hand of His people Israel. The Philistines dealt by revenge with a despiteful heart for old hatred, so the Cherethims are cut off and the remnant of the sea coast destroyed.",
    context:"Four short oracles against the Ammonites, Moab, Edom, and the Philistines, each punished for its treatment of Judah in her fall.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 25:3", text:"And say unto the Ammonites, Hear the word of the Lord GOD; Thus saith the Lord GOD; Because thou saidst, Aha, against my sanctuary, when it was profaned; and against the land of Israel, when it was desolate; and against the house of Judah, when they went into captivity;" },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 25:8", text:"Thus saith the Lord GOD; Because that Moab and Seir do say, Behold, the house of Judah is like unto all the heathen;" },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 25:12", text:"Thus saith the Lord GOD; Because that Edom hath dealt against the house of Judah by taking vengeance, and hath greatly offended, and revenged himself upon them;" }
    ],
    takeaway:"The Lord lays vengeance on nations that gloat over or take cruel revenge on His people.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What cry did the Ammonites raise when the sanctuary was profaned?", choices:["Woe unto us","Aha","Peace, peace","The king is dead"], a:1, why:"The Ammonites said, Aha, against the sanctuary when it was profaned, against the land of Israel when desolate, and against the house of Judah when they went into captivity." },
      { q:"What did Moab and Seir say about the house of Judah?", choices:["Judah's God is greater than ours","Judah will rise again","Let us make peace with Judah","Behold, the house of Judah is like unto all the heathen"], a:3, why:"Because Moab and Seir claimed Judah was like all the heathen, the Lord opened Moab's side from its glory cities, Beth-jeshimoth, Baal-meon, and Kiriathaim." },
      { q:"What sin did Edom and the Philistines share, according to their oracles?", choices:["Vengeance with a despiteful heart: Edom revenged himself on Judah, and the Philistines acted from old hatred","They refused to trade with Jerusalem","They worshiped only the true God","They sheltered Jewish refugees"], a:0, why:"Edom dealt against Judah by taking vengeance and greatly offended; the Philistines dealt by revenge with a despiteful heart to destroy for the old hatred, so the Lord laid His vengeance on both." }
    ] },
  { n:26, summary:"In the eleventh year, the word comes against Tyrus, the sea-trading city that rejoiced over Jerusalem's fall, saying she would be replenished now that the gates of the people were broken. Therefore many nations come up against Tyre like the waves of the sea: walls destroyed, towers broken, dust scraped away until she is like the top of a rock, a place for spreading nets in the midst of the sea. Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon, a king of kings, comes from the north with horses and chariots, slays her daughters in the field, raises forts and mounts, breaks her towers with engines and axes, treads her streets, and makes a spoil of her riches; her stones and timber are laid in the water, and the noise of her songs ceases. The isles shake at her fall, the princes of the sea lament her from their thrones, the deep covers her, and she becomes a terror never to be found again.",
    context:"Dated to the eleventh year, in the first day of the month, an oracle against Tyrus for saying Aha over Jerusalem's fall, with Nebuchadrezzar named as the besieger.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 26:2", text:"Son of man, because that Tyrus hath said against Jerusalem, Aha, she is broken that was the gates of the people: she is turned unto me: I shall be replenished, now she is laid waste:" },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 26:3", text:"Therefore thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I am against thee, O Tyrus, and will cause many nations to come up against thee, as the sea causeth his waves to come up." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 26:21", text:"I will make thee a terror, and thou shalt be no more: though thou be sought for, yet shalt thou never be found again, saith the Lord GOD." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Profiting from another's calamity invites judgment; no fortress of wealth can stand when the Lord says built no more.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What did Tyrus say when Jerusalem fell?", choices:["Aha, she is broken that was the gates of the people: I shall be replenished, now she is laid waste","Woe to the bloody city","Who is like Jacob?","The Lord has spoken in righteousness"], a:0, why:"Tyre rejoiced that Jerusalem, the gates of the people, was broken and expected to be replenished by the trade that came her way." },
      { q:"Which king does the Lord name to besiege Tyre, and how is he described?", choices:["Pharaoh Necho, with a fleet of ships","Cyrus of Persia, with an alliance of isles","Nebuchadrezzar of Babylon, a king of kings, coming from the north","Sennacherib of Assyria, with chariots of iron"], a:2, why:"The Lord said He would bring upon Tyrus Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon, a king of kings, from the north, with horses, chariots, and much people." },
      { q:"What does the Lord say Tyre will become after her destruction?", choices:["A royal harbor for Babylon's fleet","A bare rock, a place for the spreading of nets, built no more","A fortress for the men of the east","A vineyard planted by Israel"], a:1, why:"Her walls are destroyed and her dust scraped away until she is like the top of a rock, a place to spread nets upon; thou shalt be built no more." }
    ] },
  { n:27, summary:"Tyre is pictured as a magnificent merchant ship: planks of fir from Senir, masts of Lebanon cedar, oars of Bashan oak, benches of ivory, sails of Egyptian fine linen, and coverings of blue and purple from the isles of Elishah. Zidon and Arvad supply her mariners, Gebal her calkers, and Persia, Lud, and Phut her men of war. Every people trades in her fairs: Tarshish brings silver, iron, tin, and lead; Javan, Tubal, and Meshech trade men and vessels of brass; Togarmah horses and mules; Syria precious stones and fabrics; Judah and the land of Israel wheat of Minnith, honey, oil, and balm; Damascus wine of Helbon; Arabia lambs, rams, and goats; Sheba and Raamah spices, precious stones, and gold. The ships of Tarshish sing of her, and she is very glorious, but the east wind breaks her in the midst of the seas, and her riches, merchandise, mariners, and pilots all sink together while the mariners weep bitterly on the shore.",
    context:"A lamentation for Tyrus as a merchant ship manned and laden by the nations, broken by the east wind in the midst of the seas.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 27:3", text:"And say unto Tyrus, O thou that art situate at the entry of the sea, which art a merchant of the people for many isles, Thus saith the Lord GOD; O Tyrus, thou hast said, I am of perfect beauty." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 27:26", text:"Thy rowers have brought thee into great waters: the east wind hath broken thee in the midst of the seas." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 27:33", text:"When thy wares went forth out of the seas, thou filledst many people; thou didst enrich the kings of the earth with the multitude of thy riches and of thy merchandise." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Wealth, beauty, and worldwide trade cannot save a city when pride steers the ship into the east wind.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What single object does the lamentation compare all of Tyre to?", choices:["A walled fortress","A crowned queen","A field of wheat","A great merchant ship built of the finest materials"], a:3, why:"The lament describes Tyre's builders making her like a ship: fir boards from Senir, cedar masts from Lebanon, oak oars from Bashan, and benches of ivory." },
      { q:"In the lament, what breaks this mighty ship?", choices:["A mutiny among her rowers","The east wind in the midst of the seas","A storm of hail from the mountains","The neglect of her pilots"], a:1, why:"Her rowers brought her into great waters, and the east wind broke her in the midst of the seas; her riches and all her company fell in the day of her ruin." },
      { q:"What did Judah and the land of Israel trade in Tyre's market?", choices:["Silver, iron, tin, and lead","Horses and mules","Wheat of Minnith, Pannag, honey, oil, and balm","Purple dye and coral"], a:2, why:"Judah and the land of Israel were Tyre's merchants, trading wheat of Minnith and Pannag, honey, oil, and balm; silver and iron came from Tarshish, and horses from Togarmah." }
    ] },
  { n:28, summary:"Three messages strike at Tyre's pride. First, the prince of Tyrus says in his heart, I am a God, I sit in the seat of God; yet he is a man and not God, so strangers, the terrible of the nations, will draw swords against the beauty of his wisdom and bring him down to the pit. Then a lamentation upon the king of Tyrus, who sealest up the sum, full of wisdom and perfect in beauty: he had been in Eden, the garden of God, covered with every precious stone, the anointed cherub that covereth upon the holy mountain of God, walking amid the stones of fire. He was perfect until iniquity was found in him; the multitude of his traffick filled him with violence, and his heart was lifted up because of his beauty, so fire devours him to ashes. Last, Zidon receives pestilence and blood, the pricking brier against Israel ceases, and the gathered house of Israel dwells safely, building houses and planting vineyards.",
    context:"Three messages: the pride of the prince of Tyrus, the lamentation upon the king of Tyrus with Eden imagery, and the oracle against Zidon closing with Israel gathered home.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 28:2", text:"Son of man, say unto the prince of Tyrus, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Because thine heart is lifted up, and thou hast said, I am a God, I sit in the seat of God, in the midst of the seas; yet thou art a man, and not God, though thou set thine heart as the heart of God:" },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 28:12", text:"Son of man, take up a lamentation upon the king of Tyrus, and say unto him, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Thou sealest up the sum, full of wisdom, and perfect in beauty." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 28:17", text:"Thine heart was lifted up because of thy beauty, thou hast corrupted thy wisdom by reason of thy brightness: I will cast thee to the ground, I will lay thee before kings, that they may behold thee." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Pride that claims God's place ends in the pit; splendor corrupted by self-exaltation falls before kings.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What had the prince of Tyrus said in his pride?", choices:["No king can bind me","I am a God, I sit in the seat of God, in the midst of the seas","My wealth feeds the world","I have built an everlasting name"], a:1, why:"His heart was lifted up, and he said, I am a God, I sit in the seat of God; yet the Lord answered that he was a man and not God." },
      { q:"In the lament, what is the king of Tyrus said to have been, and where?", choices:["The anointed cherub that covereth, in Eden the garden of God and on the holy mountain of God","A shepherd in the hills of Judah","A scribe in the temple at Jerusalem","A captain in Pharaoh's army"], a:0, why:"The lament says thou hast been in Eden the garden of God, every precious stone was thy covering, and thou art the anointed cherub that covereth, upon the holy mountain of God, amid the stones of fire." },
      { q:"What brought the fall of this being perfect in beauty?", choices:["A failure of his merchants to pay tribute","An invasion from the isles","A plague in his sanctuaries","Iniquity found in him: his heart was lifted up because of his beauty and he corrupted his wisdom"], a:3, why:"He was perfect in his ways until iniquity was found in him; the multitude of his traffick filled him with violence, and his heart was lifted up because of his beauty, so fire devoured him." }
    ] },
  { n:29, summary:"The word comes against Pharaoh king of Egypt, the great dragon lying in the midst of his rivers, who boasts, My river is mine own, and I have made it for myself. The Lord puts hooks in his jaws, drags him and his fish out onto the open field for beasts and fowls, because Egypt was a staff of reed to the house of Israel, breaking and wounding all who leaned on her. The land lies desolate and uninhabited forty years, the Egyptians scattered among the nations; then the Lord gathers them back to Pathros, where Egypt becomes a base kingdom, the basest of kingdoms, never again to rule over the nations or be the confidence of the house of Israel. A later oracle, dated to the twenty-seventh year, gives Egypt to Nebuchadrezzar as wages, for his army served a great service against Tyrus and received no pay; in that day the horn of the house of Israel buds forth.",
    context:"Two oracles against Egypt: dated to the tenth year against Pharaoh the dragon in his rivers, and dated to the twenty-seventh year giving Egypt to Nebuchadrezzar for his unpaid service against Tyrus.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 29:3", text:"Speak, and say, Thus saith the Lord GOD; Behold, I am against thee, Pharaoh king of Egypt, the great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers, which hath said, My river is mine own, and I have made it for myself." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 29:6", text:"And all the inhabitants of Egypt shall know that I am the LORD, because they have been a staff of reed to the house of Israel." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 29:15", text:"It shall be the basest of the kingdoms; neither shall it exalt itself any more above the nations: for I will diminish them, that they shall no more rule over the nations." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Worldly supports are broken reeds; leaning on them wounds, and God brings down what boasts it made itself.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"What creature pictures Pharaoh king of Egypt in this oracle?", choices:["A crocodile basking on the bank","A vulture circling the field","The great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers","A serpent coiled in the reeds"], a:2, why:"The Lord says, Behold, I am against thee, Pharaoh king of Egypt, the great dragon that lieth in the midst of his rivers, who said, My river is mine own." },
      { q:"Why was Egypt's judgment deserved, besides the dragon's boast?", choices:["They enslaved Israel a second time","They had been a staff of reed to the house of Israel, breaking and wounding all who leaned on them","They taxed the temple heavily","They burned the records of Jacob"], a:1, why:"Egypt was a staff of reed: when Israel took hold of her she broke and rent the shoulder, so the Lord brought a sword upon Egypt and made the land waste." },
      { q:"What will Egypt become after forty years of desolation and return from captivity?", choices:["A base kingdom, the basest of kingdoms, that will exalt itself no more above the nations","A world empire again under a new dynasty","A province of a foreign king forever","A wilderness where no man ever dwells"], a:0, why:"The Lord will gather the Egyptians at the end of forty years and return them to Pathros, where they shall be a base kingdom, no more the confidence of the house of Israel." }
    ] },
  { n:30, summary:"Howl, for the day of the Lord is near, a cloudy day and the time of the heathen. The sword falls on Egypt and pain on Ethiopia, Libya, Lydia, and all the mingled people; the allies who uphold Egypt fall by the sword from the tower of Syene. Messengers in ships terrify the careless Ethiopians, for the multitude of Egypt ceases by the hand of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon, the terrible of the nations, who fills the land with slain, dries the rivers, and sells the land into the hand of the wicked. Idols are destroyed and images cease out of Noph; fire burns Zoan, judgments fall on No, Sin has great pain, Noph distresses daily, and the young men of Aven and Pi-beseth fall. In a later oracle the Lord has broken Pharaoh's arm (it cannot be bound up or hold the sword), while He strengthens the arms of the king of Babylon and puts His sword in his hand.",
    context:"An oracle of the day of the LORD upon Egypt and her allies, followed by a message dated to the eleventh year of Pharaoh's broken arm.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 30:3", text:"For the day is near, even the day of the LORD is near, a cloudy day; it shall be the time of the heathen." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 30:10", text:"Thus saith the Lord GOD; I will also make the multitude of Egypt to cease by the hand of Nebuchadrezzar king of Babylon." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 30:21", text:"Son of man, I have broken the arm of Pharaoh king of Egypt; and, lo, it shall not be bound up to be healed, to put a roller to bind it, to make it strong to hold the sword." }
    ],
    takeaway:"The day of the Lord levels nations and allies alike; He breaks the arm that trusted in itself and strengthens whom He chooses.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"How is the near day of the LORD described in this chapter?", choices:["A cloudy day; it shall be the time of the heathen","A bright morning of victory","A day of feasting in Zoan","A silent night of watching"], a:0, why:"The day is near, even the day of the LORD is near, a cloudy day; it shall be the time of the heathen, with the sword coming upon Egypt." },
      { q:"What part of Pharaoh does the Lord say He has broken and will break again?", choices:["His scepter","His crown","His chariot wheels","His arm, first one and then both arms, so the sword falls from his hand"], a:3, why:"The Lord had broken the arm of Pharaoh, unbound and unhealed; then He declared He would break his arms, the strong and that which was broken, and cause the sword to fall out of his hand." },
      { q:"While breaking Pharaoh, whom does the Lord strengthen, and how?", choices:["The priests, with renewed offerings","The elders, with counsel","The king of Babylon, by putting His sword in his hand","The isles, by peace on every side"], a:2, why:"The Lord strengthens the arms of the king of Babylon and puts His sword into his hand while Pharaoh groans before him like a deadly wounded man." }
    ] },
  { n:31, summary:"Whom art thou like in thy greatness? the Lord asks Pharaoh. The Assyrian was a cedar in Lebanon with fair branches and high stature: the waters made him great, his height was exalted above all the trees of the field, birds nested in his boughs, beasts bore young under his branches, and all great nations dwelt in his shadow. Even the cedars in the garden of God could not hide him, and all the trees of Eden envied him, yet because his heart was lifted up in his height, the Lord delivered him into the hand of the mighty one of the heathen. Strangers, the terrible of the nations, cut him down; his boughs fell by the rivers, and he went down to the pit while Lebanon mourned. The lesson stands for every tree that drinks water: none may exalt itself for its height. Pharaoh and all his multitude will be brought down with the trees of Eden.",
    context:"Dated to the eleventh year, in the third month, in the first day of the month, a comparison of Pharaoh to the Assyrian, the great cedar felled for pride.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 31:3", text:"Behold, the Assyrian was a cedar in Lebanon with fair branches, and with a shadowing shroud, and of an high stature; and his top was among the thick boughs." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 31:9", text:"I have made him fair by the multitude of his branches: so that all the trees of Eden, that were in the garden of God, envied him." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 31:14", text:"To the end that none of all the trees by the waters exalt themselves for their height, neither shoot up their top among the thick boughs, neither their trees stand up in their height, all that drink water:" }
    ],
    takeaway:"The grandest kingdom is a tree that God watered; height without humility ends in the pit.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"To what is the Assyrian compared in the answer to Pharaoh's question?", choices:["A raging flood","A lion among the reeds","A mountain of gold","A cedar in Lebanon with fair branches and high stature"], a:3, why:"Whom art thou like in thy greatness? Behold, the Assyrian was a cedar in Lebanon, exalted above all the trees of the field, with birds nesting in his boughs." },
      { q:"Why was the great cedar cut down and cast into the pit?", choices:["His heart was lifted up in his height, so the Lord delivered him to the mighty one of the heathen","His roots diseased the other trees","His branches blocked the rivers","He was cut down to build Pharaoh's palace"], a:0, why:"Because he shot up his top among the thick boughs and his heart was lifted up in his height, God drove him out for his wickedness, and strangers cut him off." },
      { q:"What lesson does the Lord draw from the cedar's fall for all the trees that drink water?", choices:["Trees must be planted by rivers to survive","None should exalt itself for its height; the proud are delivered to death, to the nether parts of the earth","Only cedars may stand in the garden of God","Waters always protect the trees that drink them"], a:1, why:"The fall happened to the end that no tree by the waters exalt itself for its height; all are delivered unto death, to the nether parts of the earth, with them that go down to the pit, and Pharaoh likewise." }
    ] },
  { n:32, summary:"A young lion of the nations and a whale in the seas picture Pharaoh in this lamentation: he troubled the waters with his feet and fouled the rivers. The Lord spreads His net with many people, hauls him onto the open field for fowls and beasts, waters the land with his blood, and covers the heavens: stars darkened, sun clouded, moon without light, so that many peoples and their kings tremble every moment. The sword of the king of Babylon destroys Egypt's multitude and spoils her pomp, her land lies desolate, and the daughters of the nations lament her. The second lament, dated to the fifteenth day of the month, descends to the pit, where Asshur, Elam, Meshech, Tubal, Edom, and the Zidonians, all who caused terror in the land of the living, lie uncircumcised, slain by the sword, bearing their shame. Pharaoh sees them and is comforted over his multitude, laid among the uncircumcised.",
    context:"Two laments for Egypt in the twelfth year, the hunted dragon of the first day of the month and the roll call of fallen nations in the pit dated to the fifteenth day.",
    keyVerses:[
      { ref:"Ezekiel 32:2", text:"Son of man, take up a lamentation for Pharaoh king of Egypt, and say unto him, Thou art like a young lion of the nations, and thou art as a whale in the seas: and thou camest forth with thy rivers, and troubledst the waters with thy feet, and fouledst their rivers." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 32:7", text:"And when I shall put thee out, I will cover the heaven, and make the stars thereof dark; I will cover the sun with a cloud, and the moon shall not give her light." },
      { ref:"Ezekiel 32:31", text:"Pharaoh shall see them, and shall be comforted over all his multitude, even Pharaoh and all his army slain by the sword, saith the Lord GOD." }
    ],
    takeaway:"Every power that caused terror in the land of the living lies down at last in the pit; God alone endures.",
    quiz:[
      { q:"Which two creatures picture Pharaoh in the lamentation?", choices:["A serpent and a scorpion","A young lion of the nations and a whale in the seas","A horse and a chariot","An ox and a lamb"], a:1, why:"Thou art like a young lion of the nations, and thou art as a whale in the seas, troubling the waters with thy feet and fouling the rivers." },
      { q:"Which fallen nation is named first in the roll call of the pit?", choices:["Elam","Edom","Asshur","Meshech"], a:2, why:"Asshur is there and all her company, his graves about him, all slain, fallen by the sword; Elam, Meshech, Tubal, Edom, and the Zidonians follow." },
      { q:"What point does the long catalog of nations in the pit make?", choices:["Only Egypt fell by the sword","The pit holds only uncircumcised soldiers","Graves were honored for the mighty","All nations that caused terror in the land of the living end alike, bearing their shame in the pit"], a:3, why:"Asshur, Elam, Meshech, Tubal, Edom, and the princes of the north and Zidon all lie uncircumcised, slain by the sword, their iniquities upon their bones; Pharaoh and his multitude join them." }
    ] }
);
